.venv/

# Claude Code's per-machine settings. .claude/settings.json and
# .claude/skills/ ARE committed — they are how the project describes itself to
# an agent. This one is personal: it accumulates whatever permissions one
# person happened to grant, with absolute paths from their machine in it.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Source audio lives here and is never committed.
local/*
!local/.gitkeep

# Everything the tools write — transcripts, repairs, backups, test fixtures.
output/*
!output/.gitkeep

# Defence in depth: the patterns above cover the two directories the tools
# write to, but a transcript that lands anywhere else is real speech from a
# real meeting and must not be committable either. These match by what a file
# *is*, wherever it was written.
*.timestamped.txt
*.srt
*.readable.md
*.summary.md
*.repaired.*
*.bak

# The speaker sidecars, which are the most directly identifying files the
# pipeline produces. A names sidecar maps a real person to a voice; the
# candidates file it is built from carries every name-like mention in the
# meeting with the surrounding conversation quoted around it, which is a
# larger disclosure than the transcript itself in a smaller file.
# Both forms of each: the pipeline always writes <recording>.names.json, but
# `*.names.json` does not match a file called plainly `names.json`, and a
# hand-made or hand-renamed one is exactly the case defence in depth is for.
names.json
names-candidates.json
speakers.json
speakers.md
*.names.json
*.names-candidates.json
*.speakers.json
*.speakers.md

# Spelling glossaries. Nothing in one but proper nouns from private meetings —
# the names of the people and the places they talk about — so it is exactly
# the content this file exists to keep out. All three of the lookup paths
# glossary.py checks are covered, including the bare name at the repo root:
# local/ is already ignored above, the other two are not.
glossary.txt
*.glossary.txt

# Recordings, wherever they land. local/ is where they belong and is ignored
# above, but an audio or video file anywhere in this tree is a room full of
# people talking and is never a source file. Meetings get recorded to video
# as often as to audio.
*.m4a
*.m4b
*.mp3
*.wav
*.aac
*.flac
*.ogg
*.opus
*.wma
*.aiff
*.mp4
*.mov
*.avi
*.mkv
*.webm
